From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 19:19:35 +0000
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: mark the FRITZ!Box 7560 urlader and TFFS
 read-only

Two of the five NAND partitions on this board hold data that exists
nowhere else and that nothing in Linux is ever supposed to write.

nand-tffs is AVM's TFFS environment. It is where this particular unit's
identity lives -- the MAC addresses handed to the four LAN ports, the
serial number, the bootloader's own settings. There is a single copy:
unlike the NOR-flash FRITZ!Box boards, which keep a "tffs (1)"/"tffs
(2)" mirror pair, the NAND scheme has exactly one 4 MiB partition, whose
32 erase blocks all belong to that one instance. Linux only ever reads
it, through the fritz_tffs_nand userspace tool, so nothing is lost by
refusing writes -- while an accidental write leaves the board with no
way to recover its own MACs.

urlader already carried read-only for the obvious reason that
overwriting the first-stage bootloader is unrecoverable without JTAG.
Worth recording alongside it is that the same partition also stores the
WiFi calibration data, which is per-unit and equally unrecoverable, so
there is a second independent reason not to write it.

Both partitions are addressed by mtd index by anything that goes
looking, and the numbering shifts the moment the partition table does;
the flag turns a mistyped index from a destroyed board into an EROFS.

This mirrors what the two other AVM NAND boards already describe in
their device trees, vr9_avm_fritz7412 and vr9_avm_fritz7430, which mark
both their urlader and their nand-tffs read-only.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dtsi b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dtsi
index 6199a76a1fdf..66a861434fc9 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dtsi
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dtsi
@@ -33,7 +33,8 @@ partitions {
 			#size-cells = <1>;
 
 			/*
-			 * AVM urlader (first-stage bootloader). Read-only:
+			 * AVM urlader (first-stage bootloader). It also holds
+			 * the only copy of the WiFi calibration data. Read-only:
 			 * overwriting it bricks the device past the point of
 			 * recovery without JTAG/SPI-flashing the chip.
 			 */
@@ -44,13 +45,15 @@ partition@0 {
 			};
 
 			/*
-			 * AVM TFFS environment (serial, MAC, calibration). Kept
-			 * writable so the fritz-tffs userspace package can fix
-			 * up serial/MAC entries in some recovery flows.
+			 * AVM TFFS environment. Holds the only copy of this
+			 * device's identity (MAC addresses, serial number).
+			 * Read-only: it is never written from Linux, and
+			 * erasing it cannot be undone.
 			 */
 			partition@100000 {
 				label = "nand-tffs";
 				reg = <0x100000 0x400000>;
+				read-only;
 			};
 
 			/* Primary kernel slot. Written by sysupgrade. */
